    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d133">

                <pb facs="0279" n="165" xml:id="pg165"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">462.00 R 296/–: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to
                        the Ambassador in France</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                            >Herrick</hi>)</head>
                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Paraphrase]</seg></opener>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1922-10-09T16:00:00-05:00"><hi rendition="#italic">October 9,
                                1922—4 p.m.</hi></date></dateline></opener>
                <p>307. Have discussed fully with Lamont<anchor n="10" xml:id="d133-f1"/> the
                    suggestions I took up with you<anchor n="11" xml:id="d133-f2"/> as to effective
                    consideration of reparation problem by financial experts. He has communicated
                    with Morgan who will see you to discuss practicable steps.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Hughes</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d133-f1" n="10" xml:id="d133-f1-content">
                <p>Thomas W. Lamont of J. P. Morgan &amp; Co.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d133-f2" n="11" xml:id="d133-f2-content">
                <p>Presumably at Washington; Ambassador Herrick left Paris for the United States
                    July 22 and did not return to his post until Oct. 12.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
